{"body":{"text":["That is interesting. Is there any point in language being its own module? Or is it ultimately always the engine? Well, I guess it's for a store to have to depend on the engine. It's problematic. The store can depend on language. The engine can consume. So that's... But if we're thinking architecturally about how we made the engine, like if you're running locally on your file system, the point is that the engine wouldn't need to cache or hold anything because the reads would be instantaneous anyway. Of course, in the browser, there's a certain amount of latency that's understood. But again, why it's 8 rounds could be questioned again, right? And maybe if we structure what's required a little bit differently, then the expression to get everything once could be better. But the fact that the engine has to read once per expression is understandable. Maybe down the line, the engine will have a supposed cache. But I mean, architecturally, that's not what we drew. So that's a deeper discussion to be had more properly."]},"id":"1M83RHM81JC800N","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
