{"body":{"text":["Well, I don't know if we Chassis is a name from OpenLight. Well, yeah, we know we're going to want local chassis anyway, so it makes sense. Of course, you commit the other work first, but yeah, and with its session, actually, it's good to do that together. Agree. I am. Suspecting that the components themselves and how things are done isn't really, I think it's probably, I'm suspecting there's quite a bit of glue all over the place. We should do this split first, but if you can already unglue things as you do that, that's good as well. No, everything feels a little bit flat right now. You have one view and it has three modes, and you can either see things as cards, but you always see things at the surface anyway. But the point of the interface is it's kind of nesting, type matching. So what's to prevent me from getting a list with each store where each store actually shows more, right? Or where, and same goes, maybe I want to look at all the functions and I don't only want to see the surface of it, and I can actually choose which component goes there, etc. This kind of more open way, or the session. The session's a great example. I probably want to look upon the session differently than it is today. And this kind of organic, open-ended, but simple, right? It's not about the glue. It's about each thing becoming simple and powerful. The WYSIWYG thing of that. Today, the list has three buttons, and that's quite practical. So maybe the kind of way in which you switch components, or maybe there's a session is... I don't know, this is again abstract, we haven't gotten there yet, but I suspect there's quite a bit of glue, and that's why I wanted to go in here and read and review these things. But first off, it's definitely breaking away the chassis."]},"id":"1M83RHM81JC8015","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
